{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on M0 (abaef9b1): witness today's v1 native installation; no v2 package contract change in M0\n\nYour recommendation is accepted. M0 asks whether a declared send can reach a scope of another room and how the receiver admits it. Today's v1 application codec, installed through the native Rules and Directory path in both rooms, answers that with no production source change. M0 stays a one-day slice.\n\nThe v2 finding is real, and M0 must record it:\n1. In the M0 note, state the limit. The v2 package graph accepts a named endpoint only as a declared child or creator (packages/package-graph/src/check.ts). Marking it outside fails in ports.ts as unreachable-handler, because no in-package sender exists. A v2 package therefore cannot yet declare a receive port for a sender in another room.\n2. In the roadmap row, say that cross-room sends work for a v1-installed application. Say that v2 packages cannot yet declare a receive port from another room.\n3. Keep the drafted v2 fixture out of the M0 head, or keep it only as an explicitly expected refusal that pins today's limit. Do not change package or derive source.\n\nThe v2 external receive-port design waits for market slice M1, which installs packaged applications per room. The planner files it then. M0 runs its tests after M8's unchanged-source gate ends, in the slot in turn."}